This project works with the Byzantine _Chronicle_ of Theophanes “the Confessor,” the early ninth-century completion of the _Chronography_ of George the Synkellos.

We continue to produce a series of posts on this project:  
(1) [Can We Map Space and Place in Historical Narratives](/blog/can-we-map-both-space-and-place-in-historical-narratives)?  
(2) [How Should We Show a Chronography’s Geography](/blog/how-to-show-a-chronographys-geography)?  
(3) [Chronography’s Geography: Choosing Software and Structuring a Database](/blog/chronographys-geography-software-structure)  
(4) [Chronography’s Geography: What counts as Geographic Reference?](/blog/chronographys-geography-what-counts-as-geographic-reference)  
(5) [Chronography’s Geography: To Organize Geographic References](/blog/chronographys-geography-to-organize-geographic-references)  
(6) [Turning Geographic References into Maps with Recogito: Part 1(of 2)](/blog/turning-geographic-references-into-maps-with-recogito-part-1of-2)  
(7) [Turning Geographic References into Maps with Recogito: Part 2(of 2)](/blog/turning-geographic-references-into-maps-with-recogito-part-2-of-2)  
(8) [Geography and Narrative in Chronicle of Theophanes: 2018-2019 Resumé](/blog/geography-and-narrative-in-chronicle-of-theophanes-2018-2019-resume)  
(9) [Rewriting the Historical Geography of Rome with the Chronicle of Theophanes](/blog/rewriting-geography-roman-empire)  
(10) Github page with [Our Working Datasets](https://github.com/The-Travelers-Lab/Theophanes-Geography-Project)

For a summary of the project, keep reading:

![Theophanes - Chronicle - Cover](/media/projects/theophanes/Theophanes-Chronicle-Cover.jpg)

Theophanes’ completion of the _Chronography_ of Synkellos gave a year-by-year account of the world as seen from Constantinople, from the reign of the Roman Emperor Diocletian (AD 284) to the accession of the Roman (“Byzantine”) Emperor Leo V (AD 813). Particularly because its account of the seventh and eighth centuries is one of very few that survive, Theophanes’ text is one of the single most important sources for Byzantine history, and for the history of the Eastern Mediterranean world in general. Nevertheless, in practice historians tend to read the text in piecemeal form, dropping in to extract the _Chronicle_‘s account of a particular year, or event.

Our project seeks to utilize “digital” analytical tools to evaluate the hypothesis that the _Chronicle_ — despite the fact that its text is largely made up of excerpts from writers prior to Theophanes and George Synkellos — would have constructed a narrative plot in the minds of its readers.

We are now well into the first stage of our research to test this hypothesis.

Taking our cue from the earliest surviving manuscript of the _Chronicle_ (_Paris Grec 1710_ at the Bibliothèque Nationale de France, copied ca. AD 850), which divided the _Chronicle_‘s text by emperor’s reign, our project seeks to identify and then compare the geographic signals that would characterize each emperor’s reign in the minds of its readers. To do this we are currently identifying, and mapping, the geography evoked by the language of the text.

This project is directed by Professor Torgerson, and interested students should contact 
Prof. Torgerson (jtorgerson@wesleyan.edu) to get involved.

